00:00:00.287 --> 00:00:02.370
SPEAKER: For this episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.580
we're putting the
you in BigQuery.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.000
Meaning it's time
to start asking

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.189
questions of your own data.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:11.820
For today's episode, you'll
learn how to load and analyze

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.770
your own data in BigQuery.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.300
And by the end,
you'll have the data

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.060
you need to answer the
age-old question-- which

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.610
is better, cats or dogs?

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.263
For the results
of that match-up,

00:00:22.263 --> 00:00:23.430
you'll have to stick around.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.362
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.420
If you want to
analyze data that's

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.520
not already available as
part of the public data

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.720
sets program or hosted publicly
by another BigQuery user,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.810
you'll need to load your
own data into BigQuery.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:45.350
How you load the data depends
on your analytics needs

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.450
and your data pipeline.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.690
If your data slowly
changes or needs

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.070
to be loaded in a
one-time analysis,

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:56.090
you may be fine with loading
the data into BigQuery in batch.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.490
But if you need to
ingest and analyze

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.140
data close to real
time, in this case,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.500
you may need to stream
your data into BigQuery.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.470
Luckily, BigQuery has
options for loading data

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.465
that cover both of these
scenarios and those in between.

00:01:10.465 --> 00:01:11.840
Let's show you
how to get started

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.560
by loading data in batch.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.185
So what data do you
need in BigQuery

00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:17.810
in order to officially crown the
winner of the cats versus dogs

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.920
battle?

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.590
We're going to determine the
champion by analyzing college

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.540
basketball tournament
games in order

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.360
to see who wins when
dog mascots and cat

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.580
mascots go head to head.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.100
To run this analysis,
we'll need two things.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.190
First, we'll need to have the
tournament results data, which

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:37.760
is already available in BigQuery
as part of the public NCAA

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.100
basketball public data set.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.710
We introduced this public
data set in our episode

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.170
on how to query in
BigQuery, which is linked

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.970
below if you're interested.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.510
Second, we'll need a list that
has teams with dog and cat

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.470
mascots, which we have
available as a local CSE file.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.930
It's also linked below if
you're following along.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.630
To join these two data
sets together for analysis,

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.050
we'll need to load the
mascot CSE file into BigQuery

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.220
and create a table.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:05.400
No time to waste.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.510
Let's get started.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.100
Let's first create a home for
the mascot table in BigQuery.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.820
BigQuery organizes data into
containers called data sets.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.670
These data sets function
like top-level folders

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.450
that organize and control
access to the underlying tables.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.090
Let's head over to the web UI.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.610
If you want to follow
along, pause the video,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.170
click on the link
in the description

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.870
below to set up your
own BigQuery sandbox,

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.150
where you can load
and analyze data

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.430
without needing a credit card.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.380
All right, on to the UI.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.080
To create a new data set,
select the project name

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.110
on the left-hand nav and click
the Create Data Set button.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:48.110
We'll give the data set a name
and then decide on a location.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.860
In this case, we
need to co-locate

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.630
our data set with the NCAA
public data set, which is

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.450
located in the US multi region.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.270
We'll need to reference
both tables in one query

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:00.550
by performing a join.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.360
And this can only
be done with tables

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.330
residing in the same
geographical location.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.280
You can always view
the data set location

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.100
by clicking on the
Details tab in the web UI.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.830
Click Create Data Set
and the new data set

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.230
will appear in
the left-hand nav.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.990
Now it's time to create a
new table within the data set

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.660
by loading the mascot CSE file.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:24.410
Highlight the data set
and click Create Table.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.870
This dialog allows
us to directly upload

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.740
files from our local machine
up to 10 megabytes in size

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.830
and containing less
than 16,000 rows.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.080
If you have something
larger, don't worry.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.360
You can just upload it
to Cloud storage and then

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.040
select it from there.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.350
Since our CSE is
pretty small, we

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:45.260
can skip that and use
the browse functionality

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.200
to select the file
from our local machine.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.350
Give the table a name and
then define the schema.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.800
The schema is a list of each
column and its data type.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.390
We can define the
schema manually

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.050
by clicking on Add
Field or check the box

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.780
to have BigQuery auto-detect it.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.860
Click Create Table and a
load job will be created.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.810
Once the data has
finished loading,

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.360
you can navigate to
view the table details,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.740
review the schema, and preview
the data right in the console.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.829
Our mascots table
is ready to query.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.180
We'll paste in a query
that uses the mascot

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.680
table to analyze cat versus
dog tournament game match-ups.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.490
In our query, we've started with
our table of tournament games,

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.240
and then used a series of SQL
joins with the mascots table

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.130
to look up to animal
classification

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.920
for the winning
and losing teams.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.830
We then sum up the
number of wins for cats

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.400
and the number of wins for dogs
in the specific cat versus dog

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.960
match-up games.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.140
Time to run our query.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.270
And there we have it-- with 43
of the wins, dog mascots reign

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:56.350
supreme in the realm of college
basketball tournament games.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.550
The analysis doesn't
have to stop there.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.020
You can see how dogs and
cats perform on other metrics

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.720
or join this data with other
data sets to test new ideas.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.720
For example, do the
types of mascots

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.660
different between warmer
and colder climates?

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.170
Comment below with your ideas
of exploring the mascots table.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.270
We hope you look out
for our next episode

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:17.530
of "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.000
And remember, stay curious.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

